"Barclays scales Claude to upgrade operations and improve client experience UK-based Barclays Bank is expanding its collaboration with Anthropic and rolling out Claude enterprise-wide to accelerate software development and enhance operational efficiency. Barclays plans to expand the adoption of Claude Code to 50% of its developers by the end of 2026 and to the vast majority of its software engineers by 2027. The internal search assistant introduced in 2025 is utilized by over 16,000 employees and has handled more than 1,000,000 queries, helping support over 20 million UK retail banking customers. In the Global Markets division, approximately 120,000 email inquiries are classified and routed daily using Claude models, reducing manual processing and improving operational efficiency. Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier European AI startup Mistral has raised €3B in a Series D funding round led by Samsung Electronics, reaching a valuation of €21B. Just three years after its founding, Mistral raised a €3B Series D round—the largest in European tech company history—surpassing a post-money valuation of €21B. Samsung Electronics led the round, with major global investors participating, including EQT's Scaleup Europe Fund and PSG Equity. Mistral plans to use the secured capital to significantly expand frontier AI research and compute capacity, focusing on building a sovereign, open-weight AI stack centered on data and infrastructure sovereignty for enterprises and governments. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral has established a new hub in Munich, Germany, expanding its industrial and physics-based AI initiatives in Germany, Europe's largest industrial economy. Mistral opens a new base in Munich, Germany, deploying dedicated research and application teams for physics AI and industrial AI tailored to Germany's flagship industries, including automotive, energy, and aerospace. To ensure European AI sovereignty, the company aims to build 1 gigawatt (1 GW) of European computing infrastructure by 2030 and provide an independent infrastructure environment based on open-weight architectures. Leveraging physics and engineering specialists brought in following its acquisition of Emmi AI in May 2026, Mistral is conducting crash simulations with BMW and developing industrial AI with Siemens Energy. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla have partnered to integrate open, privacy-enhanced multilingual AI models into Firefox's AI browsing assistant. Mistral models have been integrated into 'Smart Window (beta)', Mozilla Firefox's AI browsing assistant. It is initially available to users in France and North America, with plans to expand to the UK and Germany later this year. It provides locally optimized AI fine-tuned for regional languages, dialects, and cultural contexts, while safeguarding privacy through 'zero data retention' by not storing conversation history on servers by default. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral and Cloudera have partnered to help enterprises deploy and customize sovereign AI models within their own data and infrastructure. Mistral's AI models are integrated with Cloudera's hybrid data platform, enabling direct inference execution across on-premises, private and public cloud, and air-gapped environments. Enterprises can train custom AI models based on vast proprietary data within a controlled environment and maintain full ownership of their data and intelligence. This partnership addresses the demand for 'Sovereign AI,' giving customers direct control over their data, computing, and intelligence. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Covers a case study where Mistral AI successfully modernized a complex 40,000-line Fortran 77 legacy reservoir simulator into C++ for a European energy company using AI agents and a numerical validation framework. Beyond simple syntax translation, AI agents carried out a large-scale architectural refactoring from a procedural language (Fortran 77) to an object-oriented language (C++). Secured migration reliability despite a lack of test suites and documentation by building a parity harness to verify numerical equivalence. Applied a structured workflow that balanced agent autonomy with human oversight alongside the AI agents' code documentation efforts. How Much of a Harness Does a Strong Agent Need for Autonomous ML Engineering? A research finding showing that when utilizing strong frontier LLMs, complex and elaborate harnesses (agent frameworks) do not offer distinct advantages over simple, minimal-harness coding agents. Under an equal time budget and the same frontier LLM backbone, open-source state-of-the-art multi-agent orchestration and complex harnesses showed no performance advantage over a single session of a minimal-harness coding agent baseline. A large-scale systematic ablation study revealed that the primary driver for agent performance is the LLM backbone itself, rather than sophisticated harness architectures. The study concludes that under current machine learning engineering (MLE) benchmarks, the effort to build complex hand-crafted harnesses around powerful models yields low returns on investment. Fall Into 25 New Games on GeForce NOW This October NVIDIA has unveiled a lineup of 25 new games and exclusive member benefits coming to its GeForce NOW cloud gaming service throughout October. A total of 25 new games will be added to GeForce NOW throughout October, with six games immediately available for streaming this week, including The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt – Remastered. Performance and Ultimate members will receive an in-game reward for CONTROL Resonant (Third Ice Baseball Cap). Ultimate members can enjoy high-end gaming in the cloud with GeForce RTX 5080-class performance without upgrading local hardware. Photon held a funeral for mobile apps. Now it has $4.5M to help replace them with agents. Photon, a startup developing messaging-based AI agents to replace mobile apps, has secured $4.5 million in funding. Photon enables developers to build AI agents that operate across various messaging platforms, including iMessage, SMS/RCS, and email. It is betting on the expectation that consumers will increasingly use agents rather than downloading apps directly. It secured $4.5 million in funding with a vision to end the era of mobile apps and replace them with agents. Hearing tech startup Legato launches its AI hearing glasses Hearing tech startup Legato has officially launched 'Legato Frames,' smart glasses powered by AI technology designed to amplify speech and reduce ambient noise. Legato Frames are AI-driven hearing aid glasses for adults with mild to moderate hearing loss, featuring patented technology built into the temples. Unlike conventional hearing aids, on-device AI distinguishes voices from background noise to amplify speech, while a dual-speaker system blocks 99% of sound leakage just inches from the ears. Founded by creators with backgrounds at Bose and Meta Ray-Ban, the company launched official product sales following a recent $12 million funding round. Satlyt, founded by a former Google and SpaceX product manager, raises $8M to run AI on satellites Satlyt, a satellite AI software startup founded by a former engineer from Google and SpaceX, has raised $8 million in seed funding. Rather than building its own satellites, Satlyt is developing an open software platform capable of running large-scale computing and AI models across various satellites. By directly processing sensor data and anomalies onboard spacecraft using AI, it can significantly reduce downlink costs and latency for transmitting data back to Earth. Earlier this year, it deployed Google DeepMind's Gemma model aboard a Momentus spacecraft, reducing error transmission data by over 60%, and plans to test workloads for NASA, Stellerian, TakeMe2Space, and others with this launch. Inside our months-long investigation into Kevin O’Leary’s Utah data center debacle An in-depth investigative report detailing how Kevin O'Leary's 'Wonder Valley (Stratos)'—planned to be the world's largest AI data center project in Utah—collapsed amid fierce local community backlash and political controversy. Kevin O'Leary sought to build 'Wonder Valley (Stratos),' a mega AI data center campus requiring 9 gigawatts of power, which is more than double the average power consumption of the entire state of Utah. An opaque planning and approval process that withheld information from residents sparked severe community opposition and bipartisan political fallout. The project was halted as controversies escalated, including O'Leary labeling critics as Chinese spies, leading to defamation lawsuits and broadcast network apologies. How to Use Marimo for Interactive Data Analysis Introduces how to build an interactive data analysis dashboard using Marimo, a reactive Python notebook where cells automatically update according to dependencies. Unlike traditional Jupyter notebooks, Marimo is saved as standard .py files, offering superior version control and reproducibility. It provides a reactive execution model where relevant cells automatically rerun when dependencies such as variables or UI inputs change. By integrating built-in UI components like sliders and dropdowns with Pandas and Altair, users can easily build interactive application-style dashboards without additional tools. 10 Python One-Liners That Will Make Your Code Cleaner and Faster Introduces 10 practical Python one-liner coding tips that enhance code readability and execution speed. Presents concise patterns to replace multi-line code, such as dictionary-based techniques that remove duplicate elements while preserving order. Covers performance optimization methods through nested list flattening, dictionary merge operators, early returns (any/all), and the walrus operator (:=). Specifies compatibility and minimum version requirements across Python versions (3.7, 3.8, 3.9+) along with the utilization of itertools, functools, and collections modules. Improving OCR Faithfulness via Gated and Attenuated On-Policy Distillation This study proposes GAD-RL, a technique that adaptively adjusts teacher model supervision intensity based on student model performance and local distribution to improve OCR transcription faithfulness in vision-language models. Aims to resolve the issue where vision-language models distort and transcribe deformed or abnormal text in images into linguistically plausible alternatives Introduces GAD-RL, which gates (blocks) and attenuates teacher model distillation based on student model learning progress and response group reward levels, while adjusting weights according to token probabilities Demonstrates superior faithfulness in Qwen3.5-2B model experiments by significantly improving benchmark performance compared to conventional GRPO and fixed-weight-based OPD AREX-2: Advancing Self-Improving Agents through Long-Horizon Reflective Tasks This research introduces AREX-2, a self-improving LLM agent that autonomously refines its solutions at test time through continuous reflection and long-horizon execution. Defines self-improvement capability as the ability to iteratively refine solutions at test time, proposing reflection and long-horizon execution as core pillars. Synthesizes long-horizon improvement trajectory data in machine learning and algorithmic programming domains with verifiable feedback, utilizing it for training. Demonstrates continuous performance gains as the iterative round budget increases, while showing strong transfer performance across in-depth research benchmarks. MoFlow: Multi-Objective Agentic Workflow Generation This study proposes MoFlow, an agentic workflow generation framework that flexibly co-optimizes multiple objectives such as accuracy, cost, and latency simultaneously without retraining. It resolves the limitations of existing workflow generation techniques, which relied on fixed single-weight optimization and required retraining whenever objective preferences changed. It formulates workflow generation as a Multi-Objective Markov Decision Process (MOMDP) and introduces Convex-Hull Monte Carlo Tree Search (Convex-Hull MCTS) to derive a Pareto-optimal set. With just a single search, it can instantly retrieve and return the optimal workflow tailored to diverse user preferences. AI Agents are Vulnerable to Radicalization A study identifying that in interactions between AI agents, LLMs can be radicalized through reinforcing existing beliefs (resonance) or persuading them into new beliefs. Investigates the potential for mutual AI manipulation by simulating conversations between a target LLM endowed with a human persona and an influencer LLM aiming to radicalize beliefs Finds that while both 'resonance' (reinforcing existing beliefs) and 'persuasion' (inducing new beliefs) cause radicalization, resonance consistently exerts a stronger impact Demonstrates that radicalization caused by resonance propagates to other associated beliefs, highlighting security vulnerabilities in personalized AI agents and multi-agent ecosystems CARAT: Do Materials LLMs Reason or Recite? This study introduces CARAT, a benchmark framework designed to evaluate whether materials science Large Language Models (LLMs) genuinely reason about crystal structures or simply memorize and recite answers embedded in the input. In existing evaluations of materials LLMs, the provided structural descriptions themselves often contained ground-truth answer fields, making it difficult to determine whether genuine reasoning occurred; the CARAT framework was proposed to decouple and verify this. The frozen base model exhibited limitations, simply copying and reciting the answer without utilizing genuine reasoning—repeating the relation in 95.6% of paired test cases. When applying matched supervision, model performance reached 99.8%, but removing the essential link caused performance to plunge to 23.4%, establishing that both stages are learnable regimes. Serverless gossip training of LSTM failure detectors: A matched-protocol comparison with federated, local and centralized learning on NASA C-MAPSS A study that comparatively analyzes the performance of serverless gossip training of LSTM models for industrial equipment failure prediction against federated learning, local learning, and centralized learning based on the NASA C-MAPSS dataset. Applied a ring-topology-based gossip learning approach that requires no central server to stacked LSTM failure detection models, evaluating it under matched conditions against federated learning (FedAvg) and local learning. On the FD001 benchmark, gossip learning achieved an F1 score of 89.6%, approaching federated learning (89.9%) and demonstrating a significant performance improvement over local training (83.6%). Identified that while gossip training is a practical alternative in environments with moderate data heterogeneity, network topologies with faster mixing rates are required in settings with severe heterogeneity such as multiple operating conditions (FD002). Learning from the Gap Between Pass@K and Pass@1 This study introduces GapFT, a fine-tuning technique that efficiently enhances model performance in single-sample decoding settings by focusing training on problems that failed on the first attempt but were solved within K samples. To address the inefficiency of conventional verifier-based post-training methods that redundantly train on problems already solvable on the first attempt, GapFT is proposed to focus on failure-recovery data corresponding to the gap between Pass@K and Pass@1. When applied to the Llama-3.1-8B model, it achieved performance comparable to full-dataset fine-tuning using only one-third of the total validation dataset, while matching the Pass@4-level accuracy of baseline models using only single decoding. Under identical training budgets and settings, it outperformed traditional uniform sampling-based rejection fine-tuning (RFT), and its performance gains were also verified through replication experiments on the Qwen2.5-7B model. Calibration-First Cross-Cohort Multimodal Temporal Learning for Transferable Asthma-Risk Forecasting This study proposes CALIBRA, a calibration-first multimodal temporal learning framework designed to reliably forecast asthma exacerbation risk even under shifting patient populations and sensor modalities. Developed CALIBRA, a multimodal temporal framework prioritizing calibration to address predictive probability distortion caused by cross-cohort shifts in patient demographics and sensor data. Integrated recurrent encoders processing multimodal data—including environment, lung function, symptoms, and medication—with missing-modality suppression gates, cohort bias mitigation learning, and conformal prediction techniques. Validated the effectiveness of the methodology on a semi-synthetic benchmark reflecting distribution shifts and missing values, demonstrating reproducibility in controlled environments. Binarization Flattens the Score Space This study analyzes the blind spots that emerge when LLM judge scoring results are binarized into simple pass/fail (0 or 1) outcomes, proposing the introduction of an evaluation system with at least three grade levels. Binarizing LLM judge rewards into 0 and 1 fails to detect scale stretching or compression, creating an issue where policies can distort the actual score distribution without revealing any change in pass/fail status. Experiments on the MATH and SciBench datasets confirmed that 14 criterion-specific distortions concealed by binarization became clearly visible under a 3-tier grading system. The authors recommend retaining at least three grading tiers ({0, 0.5, 1}) instead of binarizing, along with conducting additional external validation to prevent score illusions caused by issues such as sycophancy. HeadGuard: Selective Head Protection for Low-Bit VLM KV-Cache Quantization This study investigates HeadGuard technology, which selectively protects sensitive heads with high precision to prevent accuracy degradation occurring during low-bit KV-cache quantization in vision-language models (VLMs). While low-bit KV-cache quantization saves memory, it causes a significant drop in the accuracy of vision-language models (VLMs). HeadGuard computes image sensitivity and output sensitivity scores offline to select key physical KV heads and protects them with high-precision masks. By maintaining the image keys and optionally values of selected heads in bfloat16 (BF16), it substantially recovers performance loss without replacing existing quantizers. Large Language Models are Approximate Survival Estimators This study evaluates whether pretrained large language models (LLMs) can estimate cancer patient survival times to a certain degree without specialized training, and analyzes the limitations of their clinical application. Developed the Survprompt framework, which converts structured patient clinical variables into clinical text to prompt zero-shot survival predictions from LLMs. Frontier LLMs such as GPT-5.6-Sol achieved individual survival time estimates (cMAE) within 10% of specialized trained RSF models for specific cancer types, or even outperformed them. Significant limitations remain for actual clinical application due to uneven prediction accuracy across cancer types and institutions, as well as low discriminative ability (c-index) in distinguishing between high-risk and low-risk groups. TomasuLLM: Out-of-Order Speculative Execution for LLM Agents A study on the TomasuLLM runtime, which speculatively executes future actions out-of-order prior to verification to reduce long tool execution latency in LLM coding agents. Borrows the out-of-order processor architecture to address the issue of agents idling during time-consuming tool calls such as compilers and tests. Drafts future actions in advance, executes them in isolated Copy-on-Write (CoW) sandboxes, tracks dependencies, and safely commits them in their original program order following verification. Evaluation across diverse benchmarks demonstrated a 1.27x to 1.35x speedup in execution without any loss of accuracy (zero false accepts). Automatic estimation of verbal fluency index in people with Motor Neuron Disease using ASR alignment and pause modelling This study presents an automated estimation of the Verbal Fluency Index (VFI) in patients with motor neuron disease using speech recognition alignment and pause modeling techniques. To monitor cognitive decline even amid comorbid speech impairments in patients with motor neuron disease (MND), an automated Verbal Fluency Index (VFI) estimation system combining WhisperX (ASR) and Silero (VAD) was proposed. An approach utilizing clinically interpretable features demonstrated superior performance compared to conventional acoustic features and self-supervised learning embedding-based methods. Regression evaluation results demonstrated strong performance for P-words (R2 0.9, NRMSE 0.05) and S-words (R2 0.8, NRMSE 0.08), validating the feasibility of automated assessment. Conformal Factuality Control for Multi-Hop Retrieval-Augmented Generation This study analyzes the effectiveness and limitations of claim-level factuality control using conformal prediction techniques in multi-hop retrieval-augmented generation (Multi-Hop RAG) environments requiring multi-step reasoning. The study evaluated the feasibility of guaranteeing factuality by applying split-conformal claim filtering to multi-hop RAG settings where retrieval and reasoning occur across multiple steps. In experiments on HotpotQA, Natural Questions, and TriviaQA datasets using Llama 3.1 8B and GPT-4o-mini models, setting a strict target (95%) substantially improved the proportion of factually supported responses from 55.60%-76.03% to 95.80%-97.20%. As factuality improved, selective exclusion intensified: only 4.41%-31.09% of generated claims were retained and non-empty valid response rates dropped to 9.70%-51.40% under the 95% target, highlighting the critical trade-off between reliability and abstention. Framing the Narrative: Ideological Mimicry in Large Language Models A study on the phenomenon of 'ideological mimicry,' where large language models (LLMs) adjust their political stance to align with the user based on the user's terminology choices and political cues. Unlike conventional evaluations, this study identified the phenomenon of 'ideological mimicry,' where LLMs systematically alter their responses in real-world user interactions to align with the user's political stance. The authors established the Poli-SHIFT dataset and evaluation framework to assess seven open-weight LLMs across 10 major political issues in the US, the UK, and Australia using multiple-choice and free-form generation formats. The model's responses are systematically biased depending on prompt framing and the user's stated political orientation, suggesting a risk that LLMs may create personalized information environments that reinforce existing beliefs. Introducing Olmo-core 3: Open, scalable training infrastructure for large MoEs The Allen Institute for AI has unveiled Olmo-core 3, an open-source training infrastructure redesigned to efficiently support the training of MoE models at the trillion-parameter scale. Olmo-core 3 is a large-scale MoE training framework redesigned to scale up to trillion-parameter levels while maintaining computational efficiency. By adopting a DDP-based approach instead of the traditional FSDP-based method, it keeps experts resident in GPU memory and routes data to them, eliminating the overhead of repetitive weight gathering. It demonstrated massive scalability by keeping throughput degradation under 5% even when total parameters increased by more than tenfold from 4.6B to 47B. StreetComplete iOS Version Launches Public Beta StreetComplete, an OpenStreetMap contributor app for Android, has launched a TestFlight public beta for its iOS version using Kotlin Multiplatform and Compose Multiplatform. To preserve existing 100% Kotlin code as much as possible, Kotlin Multiplatform and Compose Multiplatform were adopted instead of a Flutter rewrite to port app logic and UI to iOS. The existing Android XML layout and Fragment architecture were gradually transitioned toward Composable and ViewModel patterns, and dependencies were replaced with multiplatform libraries such as MapLibre-native. The released initial iOS beta build has some limitations, such as the map not working on iOS 15.x, and related issues remain open to collect feedback until the official release. Halfspace - An Experimental IDE for Distance Field-Based Solid Modeling Introduces Halfspace, an experimental IDE based on the Fidget geometry kernel that visualizes distance fields and enables creating 3D solid models using scripts and primitive shapes. Beyond simple shape composition, Halfspace is an IDE that visualizes the distance field itself, allowing users to intuitively identify geometric issues such as discontinuities and normal errors. By improving the Fidget geometry kernel to support GPU-based rasterization and post-processing, it delivers real-time rendering performance across both web (WebGPU) and native environments. Adopting an implicit surface-based approach compared to conventional Boundary Representation (B-rep) CAD, it is easier for small-scale developers to manage and focuses on practical, manufacturable modeling geared toward 3D printing. Show GN: query-inspector - A Claude Code Skill That Extracts SQL/ORM Queries from Source Code and Generates Tuning Reports Introducing query-inspector, a Claude Code skill that extracts SQL and ORM queries from source code, diagnoses anti-patterns such as missing indexes and N+1 issues, and provides tuning reports. Extracts various ORM and SQL queries from project source code, including JPA derived query methods, @Query, QueryDSL, Kotlin JDSL, and MyBatis Diagnoses missing indexes, N+1 issues, and query anti-patterns to generate analysis findings and tuning guide reports Provides Claude Code plugin functionality supporting incremental analysis by extracting queries only from Git changes Gemini 4 Argon: A New Era of Frontier Intelligence Google has unveiled 'Gemini 4 Argon,' a new frontier model featuring an output limit of up to 1 million tokens and specialized for executing long-running, multi-step workflows. Supports sustained execution of complex coding, specialized knowledge tasks, and extended reasoning workloads through an expanded output limit of 1 million tokens, up from the previous 64k tokens Demonstrates exceptional performance on real-world engineering tasks, including quantum algorithms, datacenter memory optimization, and large-scale C/C++ to Rust migration Strengthens defensive cybersecurity capabilities such as self-driven vulnerability detection, verification, and patching, while incorporating reasoning-surveillance-based misalignment prevention How AI Got Here and Where It Is Headed An analysis of the major shift in tech investment toward capital-intensive hard sciences and deep tech, driven by the convergence of AI advancements and geopolitical supply chain restructuring. Deep tech has emerged as the focal point of investment due to a combination of drastic reductions in exploration and simulation costs via AI, shifting mindsets among founders, and the imperative for geopolitical self-reliance. A growing trend is emerging in core national security and supply chain sectors—such as defense, pharmaceuticals/biotech, and critical minerals—to move away from outsourcing and pursue domestic, self-sufficient production. While a bubble exists in deep tech, it will leave behind foundational infrastructure much like the railway and dot-com bubbles of the past; thus, long-term discipline is required while being wary of overly speculative financial engineering and adverse side effects. [D] Simple Questions Thread This is a recurring thread designed to gather and answer simple questions in one place instead of individual question posts within the machine learning community. It guides users to post simple questions in this thread rather than creating new individual posts. This thread remains active for ongoing questions until the next recurring thread is posted. It expresses gratitude to community members who answered questions in the previous thread. Parallel-in-Time Training of Recurrent Neural Networks for Dynamical Systems Reconstruction [R] Introduction of research that combines DEER with Generalized Teacher Forcing (GTF) to accelerate parallel-in-time training of nonlinear RNNs by over 100x for chaotic dynamical systems reconstruction The conventional DEER method parallelizes RNN forward passes by scaling to O[(log T)²] across the entire sequence T via Newton-based fixed-point iteration, but suffers from performance degradation in chaotic dynamics Integrating Generalized Teacher Forcing (GTF) stabilizes DEER and mitigates exposure bias by preventing divergence even in chaotic dynamics environments Achieves parallel-in-time and stable training on ultra-long sequences (T>10⁶), demonstrating superior performance over modern state-space models like Mamba in dynamical systems reconstruction (DSR) LLMs that push back on a wrong user still accept the same wrong answer from a \"verified source\" - NeurIPS 2026 [R] A summary of research on authority bias in LLMs, showing that models which refute incorrect claims made by users readily accept and switch to the same wrong answers when presented by a 'verified source'. In 7 out of 8 models that resisted incorrect user claims, simply adding a 'verified source' label caused 45–88% of previously correct responses to flip to incorrect answers. Internal representation analysis revealed that source endorsement and user endorsement share high cosine similarity while retaining subtle distinguishing features based on the speaker; intervening on these representations reduced the acceptance rate gap between sources and users by 55–61%. The findings highlight the risk of agentic models blindly trusting external tools or retrieved documents, pointing out blind spots in existing sycophancy evaluation benchmarks that focus primarily on user pressure. How to address novelty concerns in top ai conference? [D] A researcher's question and request for advice on how to address novelty concerns frequently raised during paper reviews at top-tier AI conferences and effectively highlight contributions. A computer vision researcher asks for strategies to address 'lack of novelty' feedback often encountered when submitting to top-tier conferences such as NeurIPS, ICLR, and CVPR Expresses concerns about how to establish and emphasize genuine novelty in an oversaturated research environment where thousands of papers are submitted each year Seeks advice on the criteria that distinguish meaningful incremental progress from work lacking novelty, as well as the core elements of novelty prioritized by reviewers Gemini 4 Argon - 1 Million Output Headroom. Hype or a Leap? [D] Discusses whether Gemini 4 Argon, offering up to 1 million tokens of output capacity, represents a genuine leap forward for agent workflows or merely hype. Gemini 4 Argon supports an output limit of 1 million tokens—equivalent to approximately 1,400 pages—surpassing the limitations of existing models such as Opus 5.5 and Astra (128–300K tokens). By maintaining extensive context without long-task chunking or 'continue' prompt loops, it can offer advantages for agentic tasks like large-scale code migrations and security patches. For 95% of everyday work, such massive output is unnecessary, and concerns have been raised regarding its practical utility due to the potential risk of logical degradation during generation. AI: LLMs, Agents, Work, and Us - Engineers. Personal Thoughts A personal reflective essay highlighting the risk that while AI agent-based development accelerates development speed, engineers may be reduced to passive bystanders who merely press approval buttons. Collaboration with AI agents is fast and superior, initially feeling like a reliable assistant, but the agents may gradually take the initiative. There is a danger that engineers will lose their roles as architects or controllers and be degraded to mere 'meat middleware' between the agent and the deploy button. Having directly executed commands and operated tools in the past, the author felt this shift firsthand through experiencing extreme automation across entire projects using tools like Hermes Agent recently. Who owns AI risk at work? Business and tech leaders can’t agree, PwC survey finds According to a global survey by PwC, there is no clear consensus among business and technology leaders regarding who is accountable for the security and risk management of AI and AI agents. According to PwC's Digital Trust Insights 2027 report, which surveyed approximately 4,000 business and tech leaders across 71 countries, accountability for managing agentic AI and AI security within enterprises remains unclear. Regarding AI accountability, 29% of respondents identified tech leaders such as the CIO or CTO, 26% pointed to dedicated AI leaders or departments, 17% cited the CISO or security teams, and 11% responded that responsibility is unclear or distributed. Approximately one-third of companies (33%) have appointed dedicated AI roles, such as Chief AI Officers, yet the definition of whether these roles encompass overall AI security and governance responsibilities remains insufficient. Electricity Theft Is Rampant, but Delhi Found a Fix Highlights the severity of power grid losses and electricity theft, along with the solution from Delhi, India, which successfully achieved a dramatic reduction over 25 years. Worldwide, transmission and distribution losses across power grids and illegal electricity theft are severe, worsening power supply-demand dynamics and greenhouse gas emissions in many countries. Delhi, India went beyond simply adopting technology and reduced its electricity loss rate from 50% to around 5% through comprehensive system-wide reforms, including equipment replacement, regulatory revisions, and intergovernmental cooperation. Countries such as Singapore and Georgia have also achieved significant reductions, but electricity theft and grid loss remain severe issues in many regions, including Jamaica and Argentina. TypeSafe AI Releases Jev: A Decision-Only Model That Returns Typed Probabilities Instead of Text TypeSafe AI has released 'Jev', a specialized AI model for developers that directly returns typed, probability-based decision values instead of text. Rather than generating text, Jev takes a state and typed questions, evaluates them in parallel, and returns answers in the form of Choice, Score, or Noul along with probability distributions and confidence values. It costs $0.042 per million input tokens with free output, offering a 32,000-token context window and an end-to-end latency of 70ms to 500ms. It has been rapidly integrated into major development platforms and frameworks such as Vercel, Netlify, and LangChain, drawing significant attention for its substantial speed improvements and cost reductions compared to traditional LLMs. OpenAI Disrupts Reasoning Extraction Campaign Linked to Moonshot AI Associates OpenAI blocked an organized adversarial distillation attempt to illicitly extract protected reasoning processes from its AI models and sanctioned the associated accounts. OpenAI detected an AI model reasoning extraction campaign presumed to be led by individuals associated with China's Moonshot AI, fully shutting it down on July 28, 2026. Attackers manipulated prompt interactions to expose protected reasoning processes without breaching encryption or databases, mobilizing more than 15,000 associated accounts. OpenAI closed a vulnerable pathway that allowed reconstructing others' encrypted reasoning via replay and deployed additional security measures, including streaming output inspection. Google Rolls Out Gemini 4 Argon to Trusted Cyber Defenders, Plans Guardrail-Free Version Google has released 'Gemini 4 Argon,' its latest frontier AI model equipped with advanced vulnerability discovery and defense capabilities, to trusted cyber defenders. Gemini 4 Argon delivers frontier performance in complex workflows across software engineering, enterprise knowledge work, and cybersecurity defense, enabling autonomous detection, verification, and patching of vulnerabilities. Compared to the previous Gemini 3.8 Flash Cyber model, it demonstrates significant improvements in attack surface exploration and PoC generation capabilities, ranking first on Gray Swan's Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI) benchmark. Google plans to provide a cyber guardrail-free version so that internal teams and trusted defenders can fully leverage its capabilities, while deploying mitigations to monitor and prevent misalignment. Apple CoreGraphics PoC Emerges as WhatsApp PDF Checks Hint at Possible Delivery Path The first public PoC has been released for the Apple CoreGraphics vulnerability (CVE-2026-86950), which is known to have been exploited in sophisticated attacks targeting specific individuals. Security researchers have released a PoC for an Apple CoreGraphics vulnerability (CVE-2026-86950) that crashes unpatched iPhones and Macs via a malicious PDF containing crafted fonts. The flaw is a bug where a calculation error during glyph coordinate transformation in the rasterizer function causes a buffer overflow and a controllable out-of-bounds write (OOB Write). Apple released a patch on September 28, acknowledging that it may have been used in sophisticated targeted attacks on versions prior to iOS 27, and CISA added it to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog. Citrix NetScaler Post-Exploitation Payload Creates Superuser, Maps Web Shell to CSS-Like URLs Attackers are conducting sophisticated post-exploitation attacks by weaponizing a critical command injection vulnerability in Citrix NetScaler to create superuser accounts and disguise web shells as legitimate CSS URLs. Attackers compromised systems by weaponizing CVE-2026-88771 (CVSS 9.5), a pre-authentication command injection vulnerability, embedding malicious usernames into authentication events. Through a secondary payload, they created a superuser account named sec_monitor, compressed NetScaler configuration data, exfiltrated it to an external IP, and deleted associated traces. They maintained remote command execution by deploying a PHP web shell and modifying web server configurations to map it to appear like legitimate NetScaler CSS resource paths. Lablup: \"The Era of Measuring AI Intelligence… Building Infrastructure for One Agent per Person\" At its annual conference, Lablup presented a 'composable' strategy that combines and operates AI infrastructure, along with intelligence measurement standards, announcing its vision to build infrastructure for one agent per person. Lablup unveiled a composable infrastructure strategy to flexibly combine and operate models, semiconductors, and infrastructure, alongside a new AI intelligence measurement framework based on 'tokens, power, and intellect.' It introduced its lineup including Backend.AI, Continuum Router, MLXcel, and 'Nubim,' a new solution designed to prevent agent resource contention through a microVM and container architecture. Drawing on 11 years of accumulated infrastructure experience, the company is focusing on establishing agent environments and is currently conducting public offering procedures for a KOSDAQ listing. \"Blocking Satellite and Robot Controls\"... Developers Abandon OpenAI and Anthropic Over 'Safety False Positives' Developers frustrated by excessive false positives in AI safety guardrails that block legitimate development tasks are migrating to open-source models. While the adoption of AI coding agents has become widespread, cases where legitimate and defensive tasks—such as robotics, satellite control simulations, and cybersecurity—are mistakenly flagged as hazardous requests and blocked are increasing. OpenAI and Anthropic have acknowledged the issue of false positives in their safety mechanisms and are making adjustments to reduce unnecessary intervention, yet restrictions on some normal workflows persist. Exhausted by repeated refusals and reduced productivity, developers are canceling their subscriptions and shifting to open-source and alternative models that support local execution and custom tuning. NVIDIA Unveils 'Physis-Lang', a Video Generation Framework That Learns the Laws of Physics NVIDIA has unveiled 'Physis-Lang', an open, language-based framework designed to help video generation AI accurately understand and reflect the laws of physics. Uses language itself instead of visual or numerical signals to represent the physical world, generating detailed captions covering causes of phenomena, laws of physics, interactions, and temporal changes Applies an agentic loop approach where a critic agent detects physical errors to evolve captioning instructions, alongside targeted data augmentation for deficient physics domains Significantly improved benchmark scores when applied to the open-source model Cosmos-3 Nano, surpassing Google's large-scale commercial model Veo 3.1 Transferred reasoning capabilities to a 4B parameter vision-language model, drastically reducing costs compared to using commercial models OpenClaw Unveils 'OCE', an Enterprise AI Agent Control Platform OpenClaw, an open-source AI agent, has unveiled 'OpenClaw Enterprise (OCE)', an open-source control platform that enables centralized management of security, permissions, and deployment for AI agents in enterprise environments. Released 'OpenClaw Enterprise (OCE)' for free under the MIT License, providing security, permission management, auditing, and infrastructure control features to safely operate persistent AI agents in enterprise settings Aimed at being 'Kubernetes for agents', it offers multi-tenancy, workload isolation, sandboxing, and centralized lifecycle management without vendor or model lock-in Originated as an internal project at OpenAI and donated to the OpenClaw Foundation, it is currently collaborating with Red Hat and NVIDIA, with plans to release version 1.0 within this year Inception Launches Voice Agent 'Mercury Voice'… Breaks Latency Limits with 'Diffusion Model' Inception has launched 'Mercury Voice', an enterprise-specialized voice agent model that significantly cuts latency by adopting a diffusion-based language model architecture. By applying a diffusion model that processes full context and tokens in parallel instead of conventional sequential token generation, it achieves fast response speeds even in complex tool-calling and long-prompt environments. Recording a median Time to First Action Token (TTFAT) under 320ms and top 5% latency around 750ms, it demonstrated more than 2x faster speeds compared to major competing models alongside superior benchmark performance. Priced at $0.40 per 1 million input tokens and $1.50 per 1 million output tokens, it supports OpenAI-compatible endpoints and integration with major existing voice frameworks. Chinese Robotic Hand Successfully Learns Suzhou Embroidery: AgileLink Surpasses 15,000 Shipments of Dexterous Robotic Hands A robotic hand developed by Chinese robotics firm AgileLink has successfully mastered intricate, traditional Suzhou embroidery techniques, proving its high-precision dexterous manipulation capabilities as shipments exceeded 15,000 units. AgileLink's robotic hand 'OmniHand 3 Ultra' mastered delicate Suzhou embroidery techniques, such as splitting silk threads and threading needles, from a master embroiderer. Equipped with 21 active degrees of freedom, a fully direct-drive structure, and vision-based tactile sensors, the device can delicately handle easily deformable, flexible materials. Spun off from Agibot in January 2026, AgileLink achieved cumulative shipments of 15,000 units and attained unicorn status with a $1 billion valuation just five months after its launch."